WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.673
Multi-GPU systems can increase model capacity, throughput, concurrency,

00:00:03.673 --> 00:00:09.032
or pipeline flexibility, but they do not automatically become one larger GPU.

00:00:09.032 --> 00:00:12.159
Software must divide models or work explicitly.

00:00:12.159 --> 00:00:17.965
Design around the requirement: fitting one model, accelerating one request, serving more users,

00:00:17.965 --> 00:00:20.644
processing batches, or isolating different models.

00:00:20.644 --> 00:00:24.664
Each goal favors different splitting, replication, or routing patterns.

00:00:24.664 --> 00:00:30.023
Account for slots, lanes, topology, spacing, power, cooling, VRAM, context, device balance,

00:00:30.023 --> 00:00:33.150
drivers, runtime support, communication, and thermal stability.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:36.276
The slowest path can dominate the system.

00:00:36.276 --> 00:00:40.742
Benchmark single-GPU and multi-GPU configurations with real prompts, context, batch,

00:00:40.742 --> 00:00:42.529
concurrency, and sustained duration.

00:00:42.529 --> 00:00:46.995
Measure quality, first token, generation, throughput, memory, utilization, temperature, power,

00:00:46.995 --> 00:00:48.335
errors, and recovery.

00:00:48.335 --> 00:00:54.587
Multi-GPU is worth it when measured workload benefit exceeds added cost and operational complexity.

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:57.714
Sometimes a smaller model, one larger GPU,

00:00:57.714 --> 00:01:02.180
or several simple single-GPU machines is the better engineering choice.
